menu "app_wx -- the forecast"

config APP_WX_ENABLE
    bool "Fetch a forecast from Open-Meteo"
    default y
    select MBEDTLS_CERTIFICATE_BUNDLE
    help
        The weather client. Switch it off to build a device that drives the panel from
        nothing but its own state -- useful on the bench, and the configuration the
        display code has to keep working in anyway.

        Selects the root certificate bundle because this is the connection that leaves
        the LAN (PLAN 1a). The OTA anchor is a separate decision and is untouched: a
        device can pin a private CA for its firmware server and still verify Open-Meteo
        against public roots. About 64 KB of flash.

# Outside the guard on purpose: app_display draws this whether or not a forecast can be
# fetched, and a symbol that vanishes when a feature is switched off breaks the build of
# the configuration nobody runs until CI does.
config APP_WX_PLACE
    string "Place name, for the panel"
    default "Dublin"
    help
        Drawn on the display. Purely cosmetic: the forecast comes from the coordinates
        below, and nothing checks that this name describes them.

if APP_WX_ENABLE

config APP_WX_LATITUDE
    string "Latitude"
    default "53.3331"
    help
        Decimal degrees. Open-Meteo takes coordinates, never a place name, so there is no
        city lookup at run time -- confirm a new location against
        https://geocoding-api.open-meteo.com/v1/search?name=... before compiling it in.
        The default is Dublin city centre (PLAN 9.3).

config APP_WX_LONGITUDE
    string "Longitude"
    default "-6.2489"

config APP_WX_HTTP_TIMEOUT_MS
    int "HTTP timeout (ms)"
    default 10000
    help
        The whole request, not per byte. A forecast that is late is not worth a task
        blocked on it: the panel already has a cached one to show, marked stale.

endif

endmenu
